WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.960
 Now, the cheapest model call is, well, the one that you never have to make.

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:07.120
 And support bots, like the one that we've built, they often get the same types of requests.

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:09.840
 If it's a support bot, maybe it's supporting an FAQ,

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:11.840
 there's a lot of the same requests coming through.

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:17.200
 So I'm here in my settings, and I can change my cache responses easily with one click here,

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:20.000
 and my gateway will start caching responses.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.120
 I have automatically purge requests after five minutes.

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:26.480
 I could change that easily to a different value or custom.

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:29.200
 So I'll save that five-minute caching period.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:31.200
 We can look over at our application now.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:34.560
 So this application with no change as a code, just a change to the gateway.

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:36.960
 I'll send this cache scenario through.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:40.320
 We can send this through exactly what we expect.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:43.600
 A cache miss as the first time it goes through our application.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:49.280
 Let me refresh, and I'll send this again, and we get the cache hit exactly what we're expecting.

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:56.960
 So our AI gateway has added a single click caching for us so that we can cache requests.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:02.080
 It's important to realize that the cache matches on each request, not just who's asking.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:04.560
 So let's look at the logs really quick.

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:08.640
 I can refresh, and yeah, we can see this was...

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:12.080
 Now we see some of these in here where the cache response is here.

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:16.240
 This little blue symbol means that the cache response happened.

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:24.000
 This is our initial request, and this is now our cached response for that same request.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.200
 And you'll see that the duration is extremely different as well.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:33.120
 We see 1,742 milliseconds versus 45 milliseconds for that cached response.

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:37.280
 So not only we're not paying for the model because we're sending back a cache response,

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:40.320
 and we get much faster responses because they're cached.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:42.240
 There's also support for skipping the cache.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.880
 There's the skip cache option here as well.

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:50.160
 So you can send along the skip cache through, and it will skip the cache for any request in case you need that.

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:54.880
 One more thing I want to look at under settings is the rate limiting requests.

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:56.560
 This is right under our cache settings.

00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:59.680
 So I could turn on rate limiting with one click,

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:03.200
 and you can set how many requests the gateway accepts any time window,

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:05.440
 and you pick fixed or sliding.

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:08.320
 Fixed resets on the clock, say every 10 minutes,

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:12.640
 sliding counts the last 10 minutes whenever a request comes in.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:14.800
 So these rate limiting options are here.

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:21.520
 So this is a cap on requests, not a cap on dollars.

